inistic methods for finding elements of large multiplicative order\n\n大きな乗法次数の要素を見出す決定論的方法〖JST機械翻訳〗\n\n  * Publisher site {{ this.onShowPLink() }} Copy service\n  * Access JDreamⅢ for advanced search and analysis.\n\nThis is a preprint. See \"About preprints\" page for more details.\n\nTo check the article posted on the arXiv has a history of withdrawal, see the primary information .\n\nAuthor (2)：\n\nHarvey David\n\nAbout Harvey David\n\n  * Search \"202650001272445390\"\n  * Search \"Harvey David\"\n\n, \n\nHittmeir Markus\n\nAbout Hittmeir Markus\n\n  * Search \"202650001279119145\"\n  * Search \"Hittmeir Markus\"\n\nMaterial：\n\narXiv\n\nAbout arXiv\n\n  * Search \"O7000B\"\n\nPublication year： Jun. 05, 2026  update date on preprint server： Jun. 08, 2026\nJST Material Number： O7000B  Document type： Preprint\nArticle type： プレプリント  Language： ENGLISH (EN)\n\nAbstract/Point：\n\nAbstract/Point\nJapanese summary of the article(about several hundred characters).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA time-space tradeoff for Lehman’s deterministic integer factorization method (https://publications.sba-research.org/publications/042021_Hittmeir_A%20time-space%20tradeoff%20for%20Lehman%27s%20deterministic%20integer%20factorization%20method.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Our second ingredient is a deterministic algorithm for finding elements of large order modulo N. ... The following result has been proved in [Hit18, Theorem 6.3].**Theorem 2.2** (Hittmeir, 2018).\n\nLehman used Lawrence’s approach systematically and searched for linear combinations of the prime divisors of N. The idea is to divide the interval [0, 1] into parts, where each part corresponds to a fraction a/b. Considering many such fractions, the goal is to find the best approximation for q/p. This implies that the value of |ap − bq| is sufficiently small such that the inequality (2.1) holds true. The shape of x, y and k is shown in (4.6) of Lehman’s proof in [Leh74, p. 641] and is not part of the original formulation of the theorem. However, it is crucial for our application of the result.\n\nOur second ingredient is a deterministic algorithm for finding elements of large order modulo N. We will need such an element in our application of the already mentioned Lemma 4.2. The following result has been proved in [Hit18, Theorem 6.3].\n\n**Theorem 2.2** (Hittmeir, 2018). *Let N ∈ N and δ be an integer such that N^{2/5} < δ ≤ N. There exists an algorithm that either returns some α ∈ Z\\*_N with ord\\_N(α) > δ, or some nontrivial factor of N, or proves N prime. Its runtime complexity is bounded by*\n\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\\frac{\\delta^{1/2}\\log^2 N}{\\sqrt{\\log\\log \\delta}}\\right)\n\\]\n\n*bit operations.*\n\nIn this algorithm, we apply the standard babystep-giantstep procedure to compute small orders of elements α ∈ Z\\*_N. If m := ord\\_N(α) is actually found, we try to find a factor of N via gcd(N, α^{m/r} − 1) for every r | m. If this fails, we know that m | p − 1 for every prime divisor p of N. Repeating this process for various values of α, we either find an element of sufficiently large order or obtain enough information about the factorization of p − 1 for p | N such that factoring N directly is feasible.\n\nThis is a free offprint provided to the author by the publisher. Copyright restrictions may apply.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSelect Publications by Professor David Harvey | UNSW Research (https://research.unsw.edu.au/people/professor-david-harvey/publications?type=preprints)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Harvey D; Hittmeir M, 2026, Deterministic methods for finding elements of large multiplicative order, http://dx.doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2601.11131\n\nSelect Publications\n\n# By Professor David Harvey\n\n## Preprints\n\nHarvey D; Hittmeir M, 2026, Deterministic methods for finding elements of large m